# Privacy and telemetry

Locus is self-hosted so that a person's location history stays on their own server. Any byte that leaves that server breaks the product's core promise.

## Never add without explicit approval

- Analytics, product metrics, session replay, or usage pings of any kind.
- Crash and error reporting that transmits off-instance (Sentry, Bugsnag, Crashlytics).
- Update checks, licence checks, or "phone home" calls to any domain we control.
- Fonts, scripts, or images loaded from a CDN at runtime — bundle them instead.
- SDKs that collect device identifiers or advertising IDs as a side effect.

Default to off, local, and opt-in. Diagnostics belong in the operator's own logs.

## Coordinates are the sensitive payload

- Never send coordinates, photos, or place names to a third party. The only exception is the operator's configured map tile provider, which necessarily sees viewport requests.
- Do not put coordinates, tokens, or user emails in log lines or error messages.
- Geocoding, search, and routing are all third-party egress — ask before wiring any of them in.

## Permissions

Request a device permission at the moment it is used, with a reason, and only the narrowest scope. Foreground location only in v1; background location is a later phase and a distribution decision, not an implementation detail.
